-- US-1985: Activewear brand knowledge, tier 2 (9 brands).
--
-- Champion, Fila, PUMA, Reebok, ASICS, On Running, HOKA, Outdoor Voices,
-- Girlfriend Collective. The high-volume athletic/athleisure tier beside 00452
-- (Under Armour / Vuori / Fabletics / Beyond Yoga), 00450-ish (Alo / Athleta /
-- Lululemon / Gymshark) and 00459 (the footwear pack), none of which are
-- re-touched.
--
-- FIVE OF THE NINE ALREADY HAD A ROW and it was a STUB: 00389 seeded champion /
-- fila / puma / reebok / asics as bare `('puma', 'PUMA', ARRAY['puma'], ...)`
-- shells — canonical + one alias, no styles, no charts, no tells, no eras. So
-- they canonicalized and then contributed NOTHING to the prompt. This promotes
-- all five to full packs. The other four (On, HOKA, Outdoor Voices, Girlfriend
-- Collective) were passthrough-only: a "hoka one one" tag rendered the seller's
-- own casing into the prompt block and the eBay Brand aspect.
--
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- WHAT MAKES THIS GROUP DIFFERENT FROM EVERY PACK BEFORE IT
--
--   1. ONE BRAND KEY, TWO SIZING SYSTEMS — and this pack is the FIRST to have
--      the problem. Verified against the shipped table: NO brand in
--      sizing-charts.ts today owns both a Footwear chart and a garment chart.
--      00459 was footwear-only; 00464 was apparel-only; the athleisure packs are
--      apparel-only. Here SIX brands (Fila, PUMA, Reebok, ASICS, On, HOKA) sell
--      shoes AND clothes under ONE name, so `puma` owns a chart where the label
--      is a STAMPED US/UK/EU shoe number and another where it is an alpha letter
--      mapped onto a chest. category_match is the only thing separating them, and
--      a miss silently hands a hoodie a shoe chart. That is why every chart in
--      this pack states its SYSTEM in the garment string, and why the category
--      lists are drawn tight.
--
--   2. THE DESIGNED-VS-DAMAGE FACT IS A HOLE IN THE SOLE. On Running's CloudTec
--      outsole is built from hollow rubber pods — the shoe HAS LARGE VOIDS
--      THROUGH IT BY DESIGN, and a brand-new pair looks, to a naive grader, like
--      a shoe worn through to daylight. Reading them as damage marks a mint shoe
--      to Poor. This is 00464's MOTHER-frayed-hem rule in footwear, and like that
--      one it lives in a style FINGERPRINT (brandPackPromptBlock renders those
--      VERBATIM) rather than a tell (buildTrustedBrandFactsBlock truncates tells
--      at 900 chars — the US-1740 rule). The pack's OTHER half of the same idea:
--      HOKA's midsole is deliberately enormous, and Fila's Disruptor sole is
--      deliberately exaggerated — neither is deformity.
--
--   3. THE REAL CONDITION AXIS ON A SHOE IS INVISIBLE IN A CLEAN PHOTO. Foam
--      midsoles COMPRESS: the sidewall creases and the shoe is dead while the
--      upper still looks new. On a max-cushion brand (HOKA, On) the midsole IS
--      the product, so a compressed one is a total loss and a spotless upper is
--      not the grade. Seeded in the charts' notes and the fingerprints, because
--      it is the fact most likely to be missed.
--
--   4. THE BRAND NAME IS A PREPOSITION. "On" is the second-most-common word in
--      English listing prose. See the DETECT_EXCLUDED_FROM_TEXT note below — it
--      is the worst instance of the US-1983/1984 ordinary-word trap yet seeded,
--      and it is verified empirically rather than argued.
--
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- LINEAGE MAP (recorded for the 00464 reason: a family resemblance reads as
-- copying to a naive model, and here it is literal family):
--   * PUMA AND ADIDAS WERE FOUNDED BY BROTHERS. Rudolf Dassler (PUMA) and Adolf
--     "Adi" Dassler (adidas) ran one firm in Herzogenaurach, split it in 1948,
--     and built the two companies across the same river. A Formstrip resembling a
--     3-Stripe is 77 years of shared ancestry, NOT a counterfeit signal.
--   * REEBOK WAS OWNED BY ADIDAS (2006-2021), which is exactly why its modern
--     style-code format is adidas-shaped — and exactly why Reebok gets no
--     decoder (see below). Sold to Authentic Brands Group in 2021.
--   * CHAMPION AND REEBOK NOW SHARE A PARENT: Hanesbrands sold Champion to
--     Authentic Brands Group in 2024, which already held Reebok.
--   * HOKA IS A DECKERS BRAND — the same parent as UGG and Teva (UGG is seeded in
--     00459). Expected, not suspicious.
--   * ASICS OWNS ONITSUKA TIGER, and that one must NOT be folded (see the ASICS
--     row): it prints its own name, sells to a different buyer, and comping one
--     against a Gel-Kayano is wrong.
--   * FILA is Italian (Biella, 1911) but has been South Korean-owned since 2007
--     (Fila Holdings). A Korean-parent Italian heritage brand is normal here.
--
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- DECODERS — seeded for exactly ONE of the nine, on the epic's standing rule:
-- tag-printed AND regular AND BRAND-UNIQUE IN FORMAT (the third test is the one
-- that is easiest to skip — US-1736).
--   * ASICS article_number — the modern 8-character article number (1011B491,
--     1201A019) is printed on the tongue label beside the size, is perfectly
--     regular, and its DIGITS-THEN-LETTER shape is not used by the brands it
--     would be confused with: Nike is letters-then-digits (CW2288-111), adidas
--     and Reebok are two-letters-then-four-digits (GY7434), New Balance leads
--     with a department letter (M990GL5). That is what earns it a decoder and
--     gives this pack its cut-tag case.
-- Deliberately NOT decoded — the two pointed refusals are fixtured as negatives
-- in brand-knowledge-golden_test.ts:
--   * PUMA's 6-digit style number (380190) is a BARE DIGIT RUN. The Lee "101"
--     rule (00454) and the Chanel serial rule (US-1736): a pattern over it mints
--     PUMA from any tag carrying six digits.
--   * REEBOK's modern code (two letters + four digits) IS tag-printed and IS
--     regular and STILL fails, because the format is ADIDAS'S — the two shared a
--     corporate system for fifteen years. This is the exact hazard the
--     brandFromStyleFormat note warns about (Converse's M+4-digits vs New
--     Balance's model numbers): a format guess overrides the tag's own brand, so
--     a shared shape must never become a decoder.
--   * Champion's RN 26094 is a REGISTERED IDENTIFICATION NUMBER, not a style
--     code — it identifies the company, appears on every garment regardless of
--     style, and belongs in a tell (it is one). It decodes nothing.
--   * HOKA, On, Fila, Outdoor Voices and Girlfriend Collective print a MODEL NAME
--     (Bondi, Cloudmonster, Disruptor), not a regular code.
--
-- TAG ERAS are seeded for the SIX brands where the era is documented and
-- tag-visible (Champion, Reebok, ASICS, HOKA, Fila, PUMA) and left EMPTY for the
-- three founded in the 2010s (On 2010, Outdoor Voices 2014, Girlfriend
-- Collective 2016) — the 00464 rule for the LA denim houses. A 2016 brand has no
-- era; inventing one would be worse than having none.
--
-- Data-only, idempotent. Every fact carries source_url + confidence and lands
-- verified=false for the US-1715 admin verify queue.

-- ── brand_knowledge ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
insert into public.brand_knowledge
  (brand_key, canonical_brand, aliases, category_focus, tag_eras, country_patterns, authentication_tells, notes, source_url, confidence, verified, updated_by)
values
  -- CHAMPION — the only true heritage brand in the pack, and the only one whose
  -- TAG DATE IS THE PRICE. Promoted from the 00389 stub.
  ('champion', 'Champion', ARRAY['champion','champion usa','champion athleticwear','champion products','champion knitwear mills','reverse weave','champion reverse weave','championusa']::text[],
   ARRAY['activewear','athletic','sweatshirt','heritage','basics','mens','womens']::text[],
   $j$[{"era":"Champion Knitwear Mills","years":"1930s-1950s","description":"The earliest tag wording. Genuinely rare and collector-tier; a Knitwear Mills tag on a sweatshirt is the oldest form of the brand a reseller realistically encounters. Transcribe the tag text verbatim rather than paraphrasing it — on this brand the exact wording IS the date."},{"era":"Running Man / Champion Products Inc","years":"1960s-1980s","description":"Tags reading Champion Products Inc, frequently with the running-man figure. This is the vintage window most Champion resale value sits in, and it predates the sleeve C-patch entirely — so a genuinely old Champion has NO C on the sleeve and its absence must not be read as a fake."},{"era":"The BLUE BAR tag","years":"c.1980s-early 1990s","description":"The single most-cited Champion dating tell in resale: a woven neck tag with the Champion wordmark set against a horizontal BLUE BAR. It marks the late-vintage window and it materially lifts the price of an otherwise ordinary sweatshirt. It is a NECK tag — if the neck tag is not photographed, the piece cannot be dated and the listing should say so rather than guess."},{"era":"Sleeve C-logo patch","years":"c.1990s-present","description":"The embroidered C logo on the LEFT SLEEVE became standard from roughly the 1990s and is still current, so it dates a piece only as a floor (not before ~1990) and never as a ceiling. It is the mark most people think of as Champion and it is the LEAST useful for dating."},{"era":"Made in USA","years":"pre-late-1990s","description":"Champion manufactured domestically before production moved offshore in the late 1990s. On THIS brand a Made-in-USA tag is a genuine era and value signal — the opposite reading from the LA premium brands, where domestic production is merely normal. It is an era hint, not an authenticity test."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"pattern":"Made in USA (vintage) / Imported (current)","note":"A real era signal on Champion, unlike most brands in this epic: domestic production ended in the late 1990s, so Made in USA places a piece before it and is part of what a vintage buyer is paying for. Still not an authenticity test — counterfeits print country tags too."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"tell":"REVERSE WEAVE IS A CONSTRUCTION, NOT A LOGO — and it is most of the price","detail":"HIGHEST-VALUE CHAMPION FACT. Reverse Weave is Champion's patented 1938 construction: the fabric is cut so the grain runs HORIZONTALLY (that is literally what reverse weave means — it resists the vertical shrinkage a normal sweatshirt suffers), with a ribbed EXPANSION GUSSET panel running vertically down each side seam. The side gusset is the read and it is visible in a plain photo. A Reverse Weave sweatshirt is worth a MULTIPLE of a visually similar Powerblend or Eco one, and the two are the same silhouette in the same colours with the same sleeve C — so the gusset and the tag are the only separators. Do not let the side panel read as a repair, an inserted seam or a defect: it is the feature."},{"tell":"THE NECK TAG IS THE DATE AND THE DATE IS THE PRICE","detail":"Champion is dated by tag wording, and each era is a different market: Champion Knitwear Mills (1930s-50s) then Champion Products Inc / running man (1960s-80s) then the BLUE BAR tag (1980s-early 90s) then the modern sleeve-C era. Vintage Champion is a large resale category priced almost entirely on this. Photograph and transcribe the NECK TAG; if it is missing or illegible, say the piece cannot be dated rather than guessing from the sleeve patch."},{"tell":"The sleeve C dates a piece only as a FLOOR","detail":"The embroidered C on the left sleeve is the mark most people associate with the brand, and it is the least useful dating evidence: it arrived around the 1990s and never left. Its presence means not-before-1990; its ABSENCE on a genuinely old piece is expected and is NOT a fake signal. Never date a Champion by the sleeve patch."},{"tell":"RN 26094 is Champion's registered number — it is not a style code","detail":"Champion garments carry RN 26094 on the care label. An RN identifies the COMPANY and appears on every garment regardless of style, so it corroborates the brand and decodes nothing. It is deliberately not seeded as a decoder. Note it is weak corroboration only: an RN is public information and a counterfeit prints it too."},{"tell":"The brand name is an ordinary word — prefer the TAG","detail":"Champion is a real brand AND a word that sports apparel emits constantly, and the collision is live: a Chicago Bulls 1996 NBA Champion tee is usually a Salem/Starter/Trench garment, NOT a Champion one, and reading the brand out of that title is a confident, plausible, wrong answer. The brand is deliberately still detected from text (unlike MOTHER/FRAME in 00464 — see the notes), so the burden sits here: when a title's Champion sits beside a team, a year or a championship, prefer the TAG and treat the word as a description until the tag confirms it."},{"tell":"Never auto-authenticate","detail":"Champion is heavily counterfeited, especially the vintage tags that carry the value — a reproduced blue-bar tag is a known trade. No serial or published authentication standard exists. Grade condition only; route authenticity to human review and never emit an authentic/fake verdict."}]$j$::jsonb,
   'Champion identity = REVERSE WEAVE (horizontal grain + a ribbed side expansion gusset, patented 1938) + the NECK TAG era. Founded 1919 in Rochester, New York by the Feinbloom brothers; the brand essentially invented the modern sweatshirt and the hooded sweatshirt. THE ONLY BRAND IN THIS PACK PRICED ON ITS TAG DATE — vintage Champion is a large resale category and the blue-bar tag is its most-cited marker. Owned by Hanesbrands and SOLD TO AUTHENTIC BRANDS GROUP IN 2024, which already held REEBOK — so Champion and Reebok (also in this pack) now share a parent, which is expected and is NOT a counterfeit signal. RN 26094 is the registered company number (a tell, never a decoder). Promoted from the 00389 stub row, which carried the canonical and nothing else.',
   'https://www.champion.com/', 0.70, false, 'migration:00465'),

  -- FILA — Italian heritage, Korean parent, and BOTH a shoe and a garment brand.
  ('fila', 'Fila', ARRAY['fila','fila italia','fila sport','fila usa','filausa','fila vintage']::text[],
   ARRAY['activewear','athletic','footwear','sportswear','heritage','mens','womens']::text[],
   $j$[{"era":"Made in Italy","years":"1970s-1990s","description":"Fila is an Italian house (Biella, founded 1911) and its vintage sportswear era was Italian-made. A Made-in-Italy Fila tag places a piece in the vintage window that the tennis/Bjorn Borg and Grant Hill basketball associations sit in — the era collectors actually pay for. Modern Fila is offshore-made and unremarkable."},{"era":"Fila Holdings (South Korean ownership)","years":"2007-present","description":"The brand was acquired by its South Korean licensee in 2007 and Fila Holdings is now the parent. Modern tags reflect a Korean-parent global company, which is entirely normal on this brand — a non-Italian tag on a current Fila is expected and is NOT an authenticity concern. The era matters only because it separates the vintage Italian pieces from the current volume line."},{"era":"The Disruptor II revival","years":"2017-present","description":"The Disruptor II reissue drove the brand's late-2010s chunky-sneaker revival, so a Disruptor is a MODERN piece even though the silhouette is a 1990s design. Do not read the retro shape as age: the original 1996 Disruptor is rare and the shoe in hand is almost always the reissue."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"pattern":"Made in Italy (vintage) / Imported (current)","note":"An era and value hint rather than an authenticity verdict: Italian production skews to the collectible vintage sportswear window, while a modern imported piece under Korean ownership is entirely normal."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"tell":"The F-BOX logo is the brand read and it is stitched in","detail":"HIGHEST-VALUE FILA FACT. The F-box (the red/navy block logo built around a stylised F, frequently with the red-white-blue stripe block) is embroidered or moulded into the garment and the shoe, so it survives a missing tag. It is Fila's counterpart to the Formstrip or the 3-Stripe: on an unlabelled photo it is the fastest way to place the piece. The colours matter — the navy/red/white block is the brand's own and a monochrome F is usually a modern line."},{"tell":"THE DISRUPTOR SOLE IS DESIGNED, NOT DEFORMED","detail":"The Disruptor II's outsole is a deliberately exaggerated, oversized sawtooth platform — it is the entire point of the shoe (the original chunky dad shoe) and a new pair looks, to a naive grader, bulbous and misshapen. Do not read the volume of the sole as swelling, delamination or deformity. The REAL Disruptor defects are yellowing of the white midsole (a genuine, price-relevant condition axis on this shoe), sole/upper separation at the toe, and creasing across the toe box."},{"tell":"Fila is a FOOTWEAR brand and a GARMENT brand under one name","detail":"Roughly half of Fila resale is shoes and half is apparel (track jackets, tees, the vintage tennis line). The size on the tag means completely different things across the two — a stamped 9 is a US shoe size, an M is a chest — so the GARMENT TYPE has to be settled before the size is read. Never carry a shoe size onto a garment or vice versa."},{"tell":"The name is Italian and the parent is Korean — neither is suspicious","detail":"Fila was founded in Biella, Italy in 1911 and has been owned by Fila Holdings (South Korea) since 2007. So an Italian heritage brand with a Korean parent and offshore production is the NORMAL modern state of this label, not a red flag. Only the vintage Italian-made pieces command the heritage premium."},{"tell":"Never auto-authenticate","detail":"No Fila serial, date code or published authentication standard exists. Grade condition only; route authenticity to human review."}]$j$::jsonb,
   'Fila identity = the F-BOX logo (stitched/moulded in, so it survives a missing tag) + a line that is half footwear and half apparel. Founded 1911 in Biella, ITALY; owned by FILA HOLDINGS (SOUTH KOREA) since 2007 — an Italian heritage brand with a Korean parent is the normal modern state of this label. The vintage Italian-made sportswear (the Bjorn Borg tennis era, the Grant Hill basketball line) is the collectible half; the Disruptor II reissue is the modern volume half. No decoder: the tag carries a MODEL NAME, not a regular code. Promoted from the 00389 stub row. RN omitted — not sourceable.',
   'https://www.fila.com/', 0.65, false, 'migration:00465'),

  -- PUMA — adidas's literal sibling, and the pack's bare-digit-run refusal.
  ('puma', 'PUMA', ARRAY['puma','puma sports','pumasports','puma se','puma suede']::text[],
   ARRAY['activewear','athletic','footwear','sportswear','heritage','mens','womens']::text[],
   $j$[{"era":"Rudolf Dassler Schuhfabrik / PUMA","years":"1948-1950s","description":"The company was founded in 1948 when Rudolf Dassler split from the family firm his brother Adolf kept (which became adidas). The earliest tags carry the Dassler name. Genuinely rare and not something a reseller encounters often, but it is why the two brands' histories are entangled."},{"era":"The Formstrip","years":"1958-present","description":"The Formstrip — the curved side stripe sweeping from the sole to the laces — was introduced in 1958 and has been the brand mark ever since. It is a CONSTRUCTION era rather than a tag era: it is stitched or moulded into the shoe, so it dates almost nothing but identifies almost everything."},{"era":"The leaping cat / No.1 Logo","years":"1968-present","description":"The leaping puma over the wordmark (the No.1 Logo, 1968) is the current mark. Its long stability means it is weak dating evidence — the presence of the cat says essentially nothing about the decade."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"pattern":"Made in Germany (vintage) / Imported (current)","note":"A weak era hint at most. PUMA is a German company whose volume production has long been offshore, so a non-German tag on a modern piece is entirely normal and carries no authenticity signal."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"tell":"The FORMSTRIP is the brand read on an unlabelled photo","detail":"HIGHEST-VALUE PUMA FACT. The Formstrip — the curved band sweeping from the midsole up to the lacing — is stitched or moulded INTO the shoe, so it survives a cut tag and a missing box. It is this brand's counterpart to the Wrangler broken-W: it is the single fastest way to place an unlabelled PUMA. It appears on the apparel too, as a taped or piped stripe."},{"tell":"THE 6-DIGIT STYLE NUMBER IS NOT A DECODABLE CODE","detail":"PUMA prints a six-digit style number (forms like 380190, 374915) on the tongue label and the box, and it is genuinely regular — but it is a BARE DIGIT RUN with no brand-unique shape, so it is deliberately NOT seeded as a decoder. A pattern over it would recover PUMA from any tag carrying six digits, which is the costliest false positive available here. This is the same rule that keeps Lee's 101 and Chanel's serial undecoded. Transcribe the number into the listing if it is legible, but never let it recover a brand."},{"tell":"PUMA AND ADIDAS WERE FOUNDED BY BROTHERS — the resemblance is real","detail":"Rudolf Dassler founded PUMA in 1948 after splitting from his brother Adolf, whose half of the family firm became adidas; the two companies were built in the same town, Herzogenaurach, on opposite banks of the river. So a Formstrip that rhymes with a 3-Stripe, a shared silhouette vocabulary and a parallel model history are SEVENTY-SEVEN YEARS OF SHARED ANCESTRY and not a counterfeit signal. The two remain entirely separate companies and their comps must never be mixed."},{"tell":"The SUEDE and the CLYDE are nearly the same shoe at different prices","detail":"The Clyde is the Suede with Walt Frazier's endorsement — the same last and silhouette with a different tongue/branding treatment and, on many releases, a materially different value. They photograph almost identically. Read the tongue label and the model name; do not call a Suede a Clyde because it is a suede PUMA."},{"tell":"Never auto-authenticate","detail":"No PUMA serial or published authentication standard exists, and the style number is public. Grade condition only; route authenticity to human review."}]$j$::jsonb,
   'PUMA identity = the FORMSTRIP (moulded/stitched in, survives a cut tag) + a line that is half footwear and half apparel. FOUNDED BY A DASSLER BROTHER: Rudolf Dassler split from his brother Adolf in 1948 in Herzogenaurach, Germany — Rudolf''s half became PUMA and Adolf''s became ADIDAS, so the family resemblance between the two brands is literal and is NOT a counterfeit signal. NO DECODER, deliberately: the six-digit style number is a bare digit run (the Lee 101 rule) and a pattern over it would mint PUMA from any six-digit tag. Note the canonical spelling is the all-caps "PUMA", which is the brand''s own. Promoted from the 00389 stub row. RN omitted — not sourceable.',
   'https://www.puma.com/', 0.70, false, 'migration:00465'),

  -- REEBOK — the pack's sharpest decoder refusal: the format belongs to adidas.
  ('reebok', 'Reebok', ARRAY['reebok','reeboks','reebok classic','reebok classics','reebok international']::text[],
   ARRAY['activewear','athletic','footwear','sportswear','heritage','mens','womens']::text[],
   $j$[{"era":"The STARCREST logo","years":"pre-1990s","description":"The Starcrest — the Union Jack-derived flag/star device — is the early Reebok mark and the clearest vintage tell the brand has. A Starcrest on the tongue or the heel places a shoe BEFORE the Vector era, which is the vintage window. It is printed/stitched on the garment, so it survives when the tag does not."},{"era":"The VECTOR logo","years":"c.1990s-2014","description":"The Vector — the angular zig-zag device — replaced the Starcrest as the primary mark and ran through the classic era. This is the mark most vintage-adjacent Reebok carries and it brackets a piece to roughly the 1990s-2013 window."},{"era":"The DELTA logo","years":"2014-2019","description":"The Delta (the triangle built from three sections) replaced the Vector in 2014 and was itself retired around 2019. It is an unusually PRECISE dating tell: a Delta brackets a piece to a narrow five-year window, which almost no other mark in this epic does."},{"era":"The Vector RETURNS","years":"2019-present","description":"Reebok brought the Vector back in 2019 for the classics/lifestyle line, which creates the trap: a Vector is NOT automatically vintage any more. A Vector piece is either pre-2014 or post-2019 and the logo alone cannot tell you which — use the tag, the construction and the model. This is why the Delta is the more useful of the two."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"pattern":"Made in England (vintage classics) / Imported (current)","note":"A weak era hint. Reebok began in Bolton, England and some early/heritage classics were English-made; the volume line has long been offshore. Normal on a modern piece and never an authenticity test."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"tell":"THE LOGO DATES THE PIECE — but the Vector came BACK, which is the trap","detail":"HIGHEST-VALUE REEBOK FACT and the most precise dating system in this pack. Starcrest (pre-1990s) then Vector (c.1990s-2014) then DELTA (2014-2019) then Vector again (2019-present). The Delta is the gift: it brackets a piece to a five-year window, which almost nothing else in this epic does. The Vector is the trap: because it RETURNED in 2019, a Vector piece is either pre-2014 or post-2019 and the mark alone cannot say which — so a Vector must never be read as proof of vintage. Read the mark off the shoe or the garment, state which one it is, and do not over-claim the date."},{"tell":"THE MODERN STYLE CODE IS ADIDAS-SHAPED — which is why it is not decoded","detail":"Reebok's current style codes are two letters followed by four digits (the GY7434 shape). They are tag-printed and perfectly regular, and they are STILL not seeded as a decoder, because that format is ADIDAS'S: adidas owned Reebok from 2006 to 2021 and the two ran a shared corporate coding system. A pattern over it would mis-brand adidas shoes as Reebok and Reebok shoes as adidas — the tag's own brand would lose to a format guess. Tag-printed and regular are only two of the three tests; brand-uniqueness is the third and this fails it. Transcribe the code, never let it recover a brand."},{"tell":"The CLASSIC LEATHER and the CLUB C are the confusion pair","detail":"Both are plain white leather low-top sneakers with a Vector, and they photograph almost identically. The Club C (Club Champion, a tennis shoe) has a slimmer, flatter cupsole and a tennis-derived last; the Classic Leather (a running shoe) has a taller, more built-up midsole with a visible foam wedge at the heel. They comp differently. If the tongue label is not legible, say which family it appears to be and flag the uncertainty rather than picking one."},{"tell":"The PUMP is a mechanism and it either works or it does not","detail":"On any Pump model the inflation bladder is the product: the button on the tongue should firm the collar when pressed and the release valve should let it down. A dead pump on a Pump is a functional defect and materially changes the value, and it is INVISIBLE in a photo — it must be tested in hand and stated. Do not grade a Pump on its upper alone."},{"tell":"Never auto-authenticate","detail":"No Reebok serial or published authentication standard exists, and the style code is public and shared with adidas. Grade condition only; route authenticity to human review."}]$j$::jsonb,
   'Reebok identity = the LOGO GENERATION (Starcrest -> Vector -> Delta -> Vector again), which is the most precise dating system in this pack — with the trap that the Vector RETURNED in 2019, so it is not proof of vintage. Founded in Bolton, ENGLAND (the Foster family firm, renamed Reebok in 1958); OWNED BY ADIDAS 2006-2021, then sold to AUTHENTIC BRANDS GROUP — which also acquired CHAMPION (also in this pack) in 2024, so the two now share a parent. THE ADIDAS YEARS ARE WHY THERE IS NO DECODER: the modern two-letters-plus-four-digits style code is adidas''s shared corporate format, so it is tag-printed and regular but NOT brand-unique, and a pattern over it would mis-brand both companies'' shoes. Promoted from the 00389 stub row. RN omitted — not sourceable.',
   'https://www.reebok.com/', 0.70, false, 'migration:00465'),

  -- ASICS — the pack's ONLY decoder, and its cut-tag case.
  ('asics', 'ASICS', ARRAY['asics','asics tiger','asicstiger','asics america','asics corporation']::text[],
   ARRAY['activewear','athletic','footwear','running','mens','womens']::text[],
   $j$[{"era":"Onitsuka Co. / Onitsuka Tiger","years":"1949-1977","description":"The company was founded in Kobe, Japan in 1949 as Onitsuka Co. and its shoes were Onitsuka Tiger. This is the pre-ASICS era. NOTE the live trap it creates: Onitsuka Tiger STILL EXISTS as a current sub-brand, so an Onitsuka Tiger tag is NOT automatically vintage and must not be dated to this window on the name alone."},{"era":"ASICS","years":"1977-present","description":"Onitsuka merged with two other firms in 1977 and the group was renamed ASICS — an acronym of the Latin ANIMA SANA IN CORPORE SANO (a sound mind in a sound body). An ASICS-marked shoe is therefore 1977 or later, which is a clean floor. The Onitsuka Stripes carried over onto ASICS as the brand mark."},{"era":"GEL cushioning","years":"1986-present","description":"The GEL cushioning system arrived in 1986 and every core model since is named for it (GEL-Kayano, GEL-Lyte, GEL-Nimbus). A GEL model name puts a shoe after 1986 and is a floor rather than a date — the systems have run for decades."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"pattern":"Made in Japan (premium/heritage lines) / Imported (volume)","note":"On ASICS a Made-in-Japan tag genuinely skews to the premium or heritage lines rather than being merely normal — the volume running line is offshore. An era and line hint, never an authenticity test."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"tell":"The ASICS SPIRAL / Onitsuka STRIPES are the brand read","detail":"HIGHEST-VALUE ASICS FACT for an unlabelled shoe. The crossed side stripes (inherited from Onitsuka Tiger, where they were the Mexico-line stripes) are stitched or welded onto the midfoot panel of essentially every ASICS silhouette, so they survive a cut tag. They are ASICS's counterpart to the Formstrip. Read them off the side of the shoe."},{"tell":"THE 8-CHARACTER ARTICLE NUMBER IS DECODABLE and it is on the tongue","detail":"The modern article number (1011B491, 1201A019, 1012B420) is printed on the tongue label beside the size and on the box. It is the ONE decodable code in this brand group, and it earns that because its DIGITS-THEN-LETTER-THEN-DIGITS shape belongs to no brand it would be confused with: Nike leads with letters (CW2288-111), adidas and Reebok are two-letters-plus-four-digits (GY7434), New Balance leads with a department letter (M990GL5). So it recovers ASICS from a cut tag on the number alone. A trailing colour suffix (-001) is tolerated but is a colour CODE, not a colour name, so it is not captured. Transcribe the number verbatim."},{"tell":"ONITSUKA TIGER IS NOT ASICS ON THE TAG — do not fold it","detail":"ASICS owns Onitsuka Tiger and still sells it as a SEPARATE current label with its own tag, its own stores and its own buyer: it is a fashion/lifestyle line (the Mexico 66), not a running line. An Onitsuka Tiger tag says Onitsuka Tiger and never says ASICS. Resolving one to ASICS would retitle it and comp a fashion sneaker against a Gel-Kayano, which is wrong in both directions. Keep the seller's own text on an Onitsuka Tiger; it is deliberately not aliased onto this brand. (ASICS Tiger, without the Onitsuka, IS the ASICS sportstyle sub-line and does belong here.)"},{"tell":"ASICS RUNS SMALL AND NARROW","detail":"The last is cut to a Japanese grade and the brand is widely reported to run about a half size small and narrow through the midfoot — so a buyer's usual size is frequently wrong on this brand specifically. It is the single most useful sizing fact ASICS has and it belongs in the listing. Standard widths are D (men) and B (women); a 2E/4E is a genuinely different product a wide-footed buyer searches for."},{"tell":"Never auto-authenticate","detail":"The ASICS article number is public and printed on every box — it identifies the model, NOT the individual shoe, so it can never authenticate one. No serial or published authentication standard exists. Grade condition only; route authenticity to human review."}]$j$::jsonb,
   'ASICS identity = the crossed side STRIPES (inherited from Onitsuka Tiger, stitched in, survives a cut tag) + the 8-character tongue article number. Founded 1949 in Kobe, JAPAN as Onitsuka Co.; renamed ASICS in 1977 — an acronym of ANIMA SANA IN CORPORE SANO. THE ONLY BRAND IN THIS PACK WITH A DECODER: the article number is tag-printed, regular AND brand-unique in shape (digits-then-letter, which Nike/adidas/Reebok/New Balance do not use), so it carries the group''s cut-tag case. DO NOT FOLD ONITSUKA TIGER onto this row: ASICS owns it, but it is a separate current label with its own tag, its own buyer and its own comps — it is deliberately left as a passthrough rather than aliased here. "ASICS Tiger" (the sportstyle sub-line, no Onitsuka) IS this brand. Promoted from the 00389 stub row. RN omitted — not sourceable.',
   'https://www.asics.com/', 0.70, false, 'migration:00465'),

  -- ON RUNNING — the preposition problem, and the holes-are-the-product fact.
  -- Canonical is "On Running", NEVER "On" (the AG Jeans / Hudson Jeans play), AND
  -- it is additionally in DETECT_EXCLUDED_FROM_TEXT — the only brand so far to
  -- need BOTH defences. See brand-normalize.ts and the content test.
  ('onrunning', 'On Running', ARRAY['on','on running','onrunning','on cloud','oncloud','on ag','on holding','cloudtec','on shoes']::text[],
   ARRAY['activewear','athletic','footwear','running','performance','mens','womens']::text[],
   $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"pattern":"Made in Vietnam / Indonesia (current)","note":"On is a SWISS company that has essentially never been Swiss-MADE, so an Asian production tag is entirely normal here and carries no authenticity or era signal. Swiss engineering is a design claim, not a country of manufacture."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"tell":"THE HOLES IN THE SOLE ARE THE PRODUCT — never grade them as damage","detail":"HIGHEST-CONSEQUENCE ON FACT and the most expensive error available on this brand. CloudTec is an outsole built from hollow rubber pods (the Clouds), which means the shoe HAS LARGE OPEN VOIDS RIGHT THROUGH THE SOLE BY DESIGN — a brand-new pair looks, to a naive grader, like a shoe worn through to daylight. Reading them as damage marks a mint shoe down to Poor. The voids are uniform, clean-edged, deliberately shaped and identical on both shoes. The REAL defects are different and do count: a cloud pod that has COLLAPSED, TORN or split at its root, and the brand's own well-known nuisance — small STONES AND DEBRIS wedged inside the pods, which is cleanable and should be noted, not graded as structural damage."},{"tell":"THE BRAND NAME IS A PREPOSITION — never read it out of prose","detail":"The company is legally called On (On AG, Zurich) and markets as On Running. Both are ordinary English: on is among the most common words in the language, and the phrase on running appears constantly in ordinary listing copy (comfortable on running days, great grip on running trails). So the brand must ONLY ever be taken from the TAG and never inferred from a title or description containing the words. Handled in code: On Running is excluded from free-text brand detection AND the canonical is the long form, because a bare On as a canonical would be scanned over every listing in the catalogue."},{"tell":"The SPEEDBOARD is a rigid plate and it must not flex","detail":"Under the Clouds sits the Speedboard, a stiff moulded plate that is the shoe's spine. It is not visible from outside, but a shoe that folds or twists limply in the midfoot has a broken or delaminated one — a structural defect that a photo of a clean upper completely hides. Flex-test the midfoot in hand and say what you found."},{"tell":"ON RUNS SMALL AND NARROW","detail":"On is widely reported to run about a half size small with a narrow midfoot and a snug toe box — the same direction as ASICS in this pack and the opposite of the Reebok classics. It is the most useful sizing fact the brand has and it belongs in the listing."},{"tell":"Never auto-authenticate","detail":"No On serial, date code or published authentication standard exists. Grade condition only; route authenticity to human review."}]$j$::jsonb,
   'On Running identity = the CLOUDTEC outsole — hollow pods that put deliberate OPEN VOIDS through the sole (a new shoe looks worn through, and grading that as damage is the costliest error on this brand) — over a rigid SPEEDBOARD plate. Founded 2010 in Zurich, SWITZERLAND by Olivier Bernhard, David Allemann and Caspar Coppetti; Roger Federer is an investor and design partner. Independent (On Holding AG, NYSE: ONON). NAME HAZARD, THE WORST IN THE EPIC SO FAR AND THE ONLY BRAND NEEDING TWO DEFENCES: the brand is literally the preposition "on". The canonical is therefore the LONG form "On Running" (the AG Jeans / Hudson Jeans play — a bare "On" is what detectBrandInText would scan over every listing), AND "On Running" is additionally in DETECT_EXCLUDED_FROM_TEXT (brand-normalize.ts) because even the long form is ordinary prose ("great grip on running trails"). A bare "on" survives as an exact-key alias only, which is safe because the alias table is a whole-field lookup. Reachable by tag, never guessed from prose. NO tag_eras: founded 2010, no documented era exists. No decoder: the tag carries a MODEL NAME (Cloudmonster), not a regular code.',
   'https://www.on-running.com/', 0.70, false, 'migration:00465'),

  -- HOKA — the rename is a real dated era, and the midsole is the whole grade.
  ('hoka', 'HOKA', ARRAY['hoka','hoka one one','hokaoneone','hoka oneone','hoka one','hokas']::text[],
   ARRAY['activewear','athletic','footwear','running','performance','mens','womens']::text[],
   $j$[{"era":"HOKA ONE ONE","years":"2009-2021","description":"The brand was founded as HOKA ONE ONE (Maori-derived, pronounced OH-nay OH-nay, not one-one) and the full name is printed on the tag and the shoe through this window. A HOKA ONE ONE marked shoe is 2021 or EARLIER, which makes this an unusually clean dating tell — a dated, tag-visible rename is rare in this epic."},{"era":"HOKA","years":"2021-present","description":"The brand shortened its name to simply HOKA in 2021 and current product is marked that way. So the presence or absence of the ONE ONE is a genuine date boundary. Note it does NOT create two brands: sellers type both and both must resolve to the same row."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"pattern":"Made in Vietnam / China / Cambodia (current)","note":"HOKA was founded in France and is US-headquartered under Deckers; it has essentially never been French- or US-MADE, so an Asian production tag is entirely normal and carries no authenticity or era signal."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"tell":"THE ENORMOUS MIDSOLE IS THE PRODUCT, NOT A DEFORMITY","detail":"HOKA's premise is maximalist cushioning: the midsole is several times the volume of a normal running shoe's and the shoe looks, to a naive grader, swollen or orthopaedic. That volume is the entire design and a new pair arrives that way — do not read it as swelling, water damage or delamination. The brand is the visual opposite of a minimal shoe and that is deliberate."},{"tell":"THE MIDSOLE IS ALSO THE WHOLE GRADE, AND A CLEAN UPPER HIDES IT","detail":"HIGHEST-CONSEQUENCE HOKA FACT. Because the foam midsole IS the product, a COMPRESSED one is a total loss even when the upper is spotless — and compression is nearly invisible in a normal photo. Look at the midsole SIDEWALL side-on: deep horizontal creasing/wrinkling, a visibly flattened or collapsed section, or a midsole that no longer springs back when squeezed means the shoe is functionally dead regardless of how the upper looks. Grade the foam, not the fabric, and photograph the midsole from the side. This applies to On in this same pack for the same reason."},{"tell":"The META-ROCKER is a shape, not a warp","detail":"HOKA builds a curved rocker geometry into the sole so the shoe rolls forward; the result is that a HOKA does not sit flat on a table — the toe and often the heel lift off it. That is the designed rocker and it is not a warped, twisted or heat-damaged sole. Do not report the curve as a defect."},{"tell":"HOKA ONE ONE and HOKA are the SAME brand, and the difference is a date","detail":"The brand was HOKA ONE ONE from 2009 and shortened to HOKA in 2021. Sellers type both and both are the same company — never treat them as two brands or split their comps. The useful part is that it dates the shoe: an ONE ONE marked pair is 2021 or earlier. The name is Maori-derived and pronounced OH-nay OH-nay; it is not the number one twice."},{"tell":"Never auto-authenticate","detail":"No HOKA serial, date code or published authentication standard exists. Grade condition only; route authenticity to human review."}]$j$::jsonb,
   'HOKA identity = the MAXIMALIST midsole (deliberately enormous — not a deformity) + the META-ROCKER curve (the shoe does not sit flat by design). THE GRADE IS THE FOAM: a compressed midsole is a total loss even under a spotless upper, and it is nearly invisible in a photo — the same rule as On in this pack. Founded 2009 in Annecy, FRANCE by Nicolas Mermoud and Jean-Luc Diard; owned by DECKERS BRANDS, the same parent as UGG (seeded in 00459) and Teva — expected, not a counterfeit signal. THE RENAME IS A DATED, TAG-VISIBLE ERA and one of the cleanest in this epic: HOKA ONE ONE (2009-2021) then HOKA (2021-present), so the presence of the ONE ONE places the shoe at 2021 or earlier. Both spellings are aliased to this one row — they are the same brand, not two. The name is Maori-derived, pronounced OH-nay OH-nay. No decoder: the tag carries a MODEL NAME (Bondi, Clifton), not a regular code.',
   'https://www.hoka.com/', 0.70, false, 'migration:00465'),

  -- OUTDOOR VOICES — apparel-only, 2010s, no era. "ov" is an exact-key alias only.
  ('outdoorvoices', 'Outdoor Voices', ARRAY['outdoor voices','outdoorvoices','ov','outdoor voices ov']::text[],
   ARRAY['activewear','athleisure','performance','womens','mens']::text[],
   $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"pattern":"Imported (current)","note":"Outdoor Voices is a US brand (Austin, Texas) with offshore production, which is entirely normal and carries no authenticity or era signal."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"tell":"The EXERCISE DRESS is the brand's one iconic product","detail":"HIGHEST-VALUE OUTDOOR VOICES FACT. The Exercise Dress — a dress with a built-in bra AND built-in shorts underneath — is the piece that made the brand and it is the bulk of its resale. IT IS A THREE-IN-ONE GARMENT: the interior shorts and shelf bra are BUILT IN and are not a separate item, so an Exercise Dress photographed inside-out shows layers that are the construction and not a second garment or a lining defect. It comps far above the brand's ordinary pieces. The name is on the tag; read it and title it."},{"tell":"The fabric name is on the tag and it is a real product axis","detail":"TechSweat (the compressive performance knit — leggings, the Exercise Dress) and CloudKnit (the soft brushed fleece — sweats) are the brand's two named fabrics and they are printed on the tag. They are different products at different prices and they photograph similarly at a glance, so read the fabric name rather than judging the hand from an image."},{"tell":"PILLING IS THE CONDITION AXIS ON THIS BRAND, not fading","detail":"Compressive performance knits fail by PILLING (the fuzzed bobbles at the inner thigh and under the seat, where the fabric abrades against itself) and by losing elastane recovery, which shows as a waistband that no longer springs back and a fabric that goes SHEER WHEN STRETCHED. None of it reads in a flat, unstretched photo. On this brand and Girlfriend Collective, check the high-abrasion zones and the stretch-sheerness explicitly and say what you found — a clean flat-lay is not evidence of good condition."},{"tell":"The brand is APPAREL ONLY","detail":"Outdoor Voices makes no footwear. Unlike six of the nine brands in this pack, a size on an Outdoor Voices tag is always an alpha garment size and never a shoe number — so there is no shoe/garment chart ambiguity here."},{"tell":"Never auto-authenticate","detail":"No Outdoor Voices serial, date code or published authentication standard exists. The brand is not meaningfully counterfeited. Grade condition only; route authenticity to human review."}]$j$::jsonb,
   'Outdoor Voices identity = the EXERCISE DRESS (a dress with a built-in bra AND built-in shorts — a three-in-one garment, so its interior layers are construction and not a defect) + the tag-printed fabric name (TechSweat / CloudKnit). Founded 2014 in Austin, Texas by Tyler Haney, on the "Doing Things" recreational-not-competitive premise; independent. APPAREL ONLY — no footwear, so no shoe/garment chart ambiguity, unlike six of the nine brands in this pack. NO tag_eras: founded 2014, no documented era exists. No decoder: the tag carries a product NAME, not a regular code. NOTE "ov" is an alias KEY only and must never be a canonical or a brandMatch token — a two-letter token in either of those false-fires (the "ag" rule). RN omitted — not sourceable.',
   'https://www.outdoorvoices.com/', 0.60, false, 'migration:00465'),

  -- GIRLFRIEND COLLECTIVE — the fabric IS the brand, and the size scale is the
  -- widest in the epic. "girlfriend" is an exact-key alias only.
  ('girlfriendcollective', 'Girlfriend Collective', ARRAY['girlfriend collective','girlfriendcollective','girlfriend','girlfriend collective inc']::text[],
   ARRAY['activewear','athleisure','sustainable','performance','womens']::text[],
   $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"pattern":"Made in Vietnam / Taiwan","note":"Girlfriend Collective publishes its Vietnamese and Taiwanese factories as part of its transparency positioning, so a Vietnam or Taiwan tag is EXPECTED here and is part of the brand story rather than a downgrade. No authenticity signal."}]$j$::jsonb,
   $j$[{"tell":"THE RECYCLED FABRIC IS THE BRAND — and the tag says so","detail":"HIGHEST-VALUE GIRLFRIEND COLLECTIVE FACT. The brand's entire premise is performance wear made from RECYCLED POST-CONSUMER PLASTIC BOTTLES (RPET) and recycled fishing nets, and the tag states it. That is not marketing trivia here: it is why the buyer chose the brand, so it belongs in the listing. Practical consequence for grading — recycled polyester behaves like polyester, so it pills and it holds odour rather than fading."},{"tell":"THE SIZE RUN IS XXS-6XL — the widest in this epic — and it is the point","detail":"Girlfriend Collective's inclusive size run (XXS through 6XL) is a core part of the brand's identity and a plus size is a MAINLINE product here, not an afterthought or a special line. Two consequences. First, the letter spans a far wider range than a conventional brand's, so an XL on this tag is NOT an XL on a brand that stops at XL — never cross-map the letter between brands. Second, the larger sizes are genuinely sought and are frequently the harder ones to find secondhand, so the size must be read off the tag and stated exactly."},{"tell":"COMPRESSIVE and FLOAT are different fabrics at different prices","detail":"Compressive (the firm, high-compression original legging — the brand's signature and its volume seller) and FLOAT (the lighter, softer, buttery one) are separate named fabrics printed on the tag, and they photograph identically flat. They comp differently. Read the fabric name; do not infer it from the image."},{"tell":"THE COLOUR NAME IS THE PRODUCT and it is proprietary","detail":"The brand sells the same silhouettes in a rotating palette of NAMED proprietary colours (Moss, Plum, Ivy, Cayenne, Smoke, Butter, Black), some of which are seasonal and become genuinely sought after once retired. Buyers search the colour name. Read it off the tag and put it in the title — a generic green loses the search that a Moss wins."},{"tell":"PILLING AND STRETCH-SHEERNESS ARE THE CONDITION AXES, and a flat photo hides both","detail":"Compressive knits fail by pilling at the inner thigh and under the seat, and by losing elastane recovery — which shows as a waistband that will not spring back and a fabric that goes SHEER WHEN STRETCHED. A flat, unstretched flat-lay shows neither. Check the high-abrasion zones and the stretch-sheerness explicitly and report what you found. Same rule as Outdoor Voices in this pack."},{"tell":"Never auto-authenticate","detail":"No Girlfriend Collective serial, date code or published authentication standard exists. The brand is not meaningfully counterfeited. Grade condition only; route authenticity to human review."}]$j$::jsonb,
   'Girlfriend Collective identity = performance wear from RECYCLED post-consumer plastic bottles (the tag says so, and it is why the buyer chose the brand) + a proprietary NAMED colour + the XXS-6XL inclusive run, the widest size scale in this epic. Founded 2016 in Seattle by Ellie Dinh and Quang Dinh; independent, direct-to-consumer. WOMEN''S-FOCUSED, so no men''s chart is seeded rather than inventing one. APPAREL ONLY — no footwear. NO tag_eras: founded 2016, no documented era exists. No decoder: the tag carries a fabric and colour NAME, not a regular code. NOTE "girlfriend" is an alias KEY only (an exact whole-field lookup, so it only fires when the seller''s entire brand field is that word) and must never be a canonical or a brandMatch token. RN omitted — not sourceable.',
   'https://www.girlfriend.com/', 0.60, false, 'migration:00465')
