-- ── brand_size_charts (INCHES) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
-- THIS PACK IS THE FIRST WITH TWO SIZING SYSTEMS UNDER ONE BRAND KEY, and that
-- is the whole reason these charts are shaped the way they are. Verified against
-- the shipped table: no brand in sizing-charts.ts today owns both a Footwear
-- chart and a garment chart. 00459 was footwear-only; 00464 was apparel-only.
-- Here Fila, PUMA and Reebok own BOTH — so `puma` has a chart where the label is
-- a STAMPED US/UK/EU shoe number and another where it is an alpha letter mapped
-- onto a chest, and `category_match` is the ONLY thing choosing between them. A
-- miss silently hands a hoodie a shoe chart, which is why:
--
--   * every garment string NAMES ITS SYSTEM ("Footwear (US/UK/EU ...)" vs
--     "Tops (alpha ...)"), so the model can see which chart it got; and
--   * the category lists are drawn TIGHT and do not overlap between the two.
--
-- THE TWO SYSTEMS READ IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, which is the fact underneath it:
--   * A GARMENT chart is an ESTIMATOR — measure the chest, double it, read off
--     the size. The measurement is the input.
--   * A SHOE chart is a TRANSLATOR (the 00459 rule) — a shoe size CANNOT be
--     measured from a photo, it is STAMPED on the tongue label and must be READ,
--     then converted. The foot-length inches are a sanity check for a shoe in
--     hand, not the primary path. Every footwear row's LABEL carries the full
--     US/UK/EU triple, because that is where the model actually reads it.
--
-- AND THE FIT DIRECTION SPLITS THE PACK'S SIX SHOE BRANDS IN TWO:
--     ASICS  / On      run SMALL and NARROW  (a Japanese last; a narrow Swiss one)
--     Reebok classics  run LARGE
--     PUMA             runs SMALL
--     HOKA / Fila      ≈ true to size (the Disruptor runs a touch large)
-- So there is no single "athletic shoes run X" rule, and a buyer's usual size is
-- wrong on this pack in two opposite directions.
--
-- HONESTY NOTE ON CONFIDENCE: the cross-maps are the standard US/UK/EU grade and
-- the run-small/run-large calls are the well-reported resale consensus — NOT
-- figures fetched from each brand's own published guide. They are seeded at
-- LOW/CAPPED confidence with the basis stated, so the US-1715 verifier replaces
-- them rather than rubber-stamping them.
insert into public.brand_size_charts
  (brand_key, brand_label, brand_match, department, garment, category_match, rows, note, source_url, confidence, verified, updated_by) values

  -- ── CHAMPION — apparel only. The vintage/modern CUT is the trap here.
  ('champion', 'Champion', ARRAY['champion']::text[], 'Men', 'Tops (alpha — vintage runs BOXY)', ARRAY['top','tee','shirt','sweatshirt','hoodie','crewneck','sweater','jersey','long sleeve']::text[],
   $json$[{"size":"S","measurements":{"chest":"34-37"}},{"size":"M","measurements":{"chest":"38-41"}},{"size":"L","measurements":{"chest":"42-45"}},{"size":"XL","measurements":{"chest":"46-49"}},{"size":"XXL","measurements":{"chest":"50-53"}},{"size":"XXXL","measurements":{"chest":"54-57"}}]$json$::jsonb,
   'BODY measurement (chest) — an alpha letter mapped onto a body, so this chart is an ESTIMATOR: measure the flat chest (armpit to armpit) and DOUBLE IT. CHAMPION IS AN APPAREL AND SWEATSHIRT BRAND ONLY — it makes no footwear, so unlike PUMA/Reebok/Fila in this same pack there is no shoe chart to confuse this with. CHAMPION-SPECIFIC AND IT IS THE POINT OF THIS ROW: THE VINTAGE CUT IS BOXY AND THE MODERN CUT IS NOT. A 1980s-90s Champion sweatshirt is cut SHORT AND WIDE — the chest may hit this chart while the body length runs several inches shorter than a modern equivalent letter, so the same L is a materially different garment across eras and the tag will never say so. Measure the LENGTH as well as the chest on any vintage piece and publish both; the era comes off the NECK TAG. Reverse Weave in particular resists vertical shrinkage by design (that is what the construction is for), so a vintage piece has usually NOT shrunk in length — it was cut that way. Standard US alpha approximation rather than Champion-fetched figures — capped confidence.',
   'https://www.champion.com/', 0.55, false, 'migration:00465'),
  ('champion', 'Champion', ARRAY['champion']::text[], 'Women', 'Tops (alpha)', ARRAY['top','tee','shirt','sweatshirt','hoodie','crewneck','sweater','blouse','long sleeve']::text[],
   $json$[{"size":"XS","measurements":{"bust":"31-32.5"}},{"size":"S","measurements":{"bust":"33-34.5"}},{"size":"M","measurements":{"bust":"35-36.5"}},{"size":"L","measurements":{"bust":"37.5-39"}},{"size":"XL","measurements":{"bust":"40.5-42.5"}},{"size":"XXL","measurements":{"bust":"44-46"}}]$json$::jsonb,
   'BODY measurement (bust) — measure the flat chest and DOUBLE IT. Champion is apparel-only (no footwear), so there is no shoe chart to confuse this with. NOTE THE UNISEX TRAP ON THIS BRAND SPECIFICALLY: much of Champion''s sweatshirt volume — including most vintage Reverse Weave — is cut on a MEN''S/unisex block and merely sold to women, so a sweatshirt found in a women''s wardrobe is frequently a men''s-graded garment and belongs on the men''s chart. If the tag does not state a department, measure and use the men''s row rather than assuming. Standard US alpha approximation rather than Champion-fetched figures — capped confidence.',
   'https://www.champion.com/', 0.50, false, 'migration:00465'),

  -- ── FILA — the first of the pack's dual-system brands. Shoe AND garment.
